<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  agbridge: the Mac side, run by launchd.

  This is a TEMPLATE. `install.sh mac` renders it into
  ~/Library/LaunchAgents/<label>.plist, substituting the six at-sign
  placeholders below, and refuses to install a copy with any placeholder left
  in it. Nothing here is generated at run time, so what launchd runs can be read
  with `cat` and diffed against this file.

  (No double hyphen appears in this comment on purpose: XML forbids it inside a
  comment, and a plist that plutil rejects is a job launchd silently never
  starts. The placeholder names are deliberately NOT spelled out here either:
  `sed` substitutes globally, so naming them would splice an install path into
  this comment, and one containing a double hyphen would render exactly the
  invalid plist the previous sentence warns about.)

  Why each key is what it is:

  * ProgramArguments: `<python> -S -E <agb> bridge`, plus the config flag and
    its path, the same shape as the hook command on the farm. An ABSOLUTE
    interpreter path, because a LaunchAgent has almost no PATH and a bare
    `python3` that fails to resolve would leave the job dead with nothing to
    show for it; `-S -E` so a stray PYTHONPATH or a user site-packages
    directory cannot change what the bridge imports.

    The config flag comes LAST, after `bridge`, and it is unconditional.

    After, because `agb` dispatches on its FIRST argument: a flag in front of
    the command name is read as the command name, refused as unknown, and the
    job then exits non-zero once every ThrottleInterval for ever. KeepAlive
    turns that mistake into a permanent restart loop rather than a one-off
    error anybody would notice.

    Unconditional, because that path is the whole of an instance's identity:
    the rows map, the placements file and the host_<name> table the row's
    `agb pane` resolves against all live beside it. Rendered for every install,
    the default one included, the installer has ONE path to get right instead
    of a conditional that is exercised only on the second machine.

  * KeepAlive <true/>: unconditional, including after a *clean* exit. The bridge
    exiting is exactly the condition the `[?]` stale rendering exists for: the
    ssh has gone, the rows are no longer being told anything, and the only
    useful response is to reconnect. A `SuccessfulExit` dict would leave a
    cleanly exited bridge down for ever, which is the silent no-op class this
    project exists to remove.

  * ThrottleInterval 10: KeepAlive plus a misconfiguration (no feed_host, an
    unreachable farm) is a restart loop; 10 s makes it a slow, legible one in
    the log rather than a spin.

  * EnvironmentVariables/PATH: launchd gives a job a minimal PATH, and the
    bridge shells out to `agtermctl` and `ssh`. This is the one thing that
    cannot be inferred at run time, so it is written down.

  * StandardOutPath / StandardErrorPath: the bridge's own report log. Under
    launchd this is the only place "did the bridge see anything?" is answerable.

  * ProcessType Background: it is a poller, not something a user is waiting on;
    this keeps it out of the way of interactive work.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>@LABEL@</string>

  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>@PYTHON@</string>
    <string>-S</string>
    <string>-E</string>
    <string>@AGB@</string>
    <string>bridge</string>
    <string>--config</string>
    <string>@CONFIG@</string>
  </array>

  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <true/>

  <key>KeepAlive</key>
  <true/>

  <key>ThrottleInterval</key>
  <integer>10</integer>

  <key>ProcessType</key>
  <string>Background</string>

  <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
  <dict>
    <key>PATH</key>
    <string>@PATH@</string>
  </dict>

  <key>StandardOutPath</key>
  <string>@LOGDIR@/bridge.log</string>

  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string>@LOGDIR@/bridge.err.log</string>

  <key>WorkingDirectory</key>
  <string>/tmp</string>
</dict>
</plist>
